digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56075-7
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes to develop and evaluate rapid quenching from peak calcination temperature as a dopant-light microstructure-control strategy for nickel-rich NMC cathodes, testing whether non-equilibrium Li/element redistribution can form a more stable surface, improve bulk charge homogeneity, and enhance high-voltage cycling stability.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Across the retained and repaired conclusions, the paper motivates a dopant-light processing strategy that uses calcination cooling-rate control to probe coupled surface and bulk degradation mechanisms in Ni-rich NMC."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Surface stability and bulk chemo-mechanical or microstructural behavior are coupled in NMC polycrystalline particles during cycling."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Doping-based stabilization strategies face practical constraints, including difficulty in precisely placing heteroatoms and increased raw-material supply-chain complexity."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Because calcination drives mass transport and typical NMC cooling rates are relatively slow, changing the calcination cooling rate is a motivated processing variable to test for modifying cathode microstructure or chemistry."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Prior Ni-rich positive-electrode evidence, together with the known non-equilibrium effect of quenching, makes quenching a plausible route to investigate for performance-relevant non-equilibrium structures."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Because quenching-related surface–bulk modulation remains largely unexplored, it is reasonable to propose testing whether quenching can reduce surface reactivity and improve bulk charging homogeneity."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Since calcination can drive cathode mass transport and conventional NMC cooling is relatively slow, regulating the cooling rate from calcination is a plausible manufacturing variable to investigate for tailoring microstructure and chemistry."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) The largely unexplored effect of quenching on surface–bulk mutual modulation supports framing a research goal to investigate, rather than assume control over, surface chemistry and bulk microstructure interactions in NMC particles."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Given the high-voltage cycling instability of Ni-rich cathodes and the hypothesis that quenching may improve surface and bulk behavior, quenching is a plausible strategy to develop and evaluate for Ni-rich NMC stability."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Increasing Ni content in Li[NixCoyMnz]O2 is associated with accelerated capacity decay and reduced capacity retention, indicating a stability challenge for Ni-rich NMC cathodes."];
  "iD" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, existing surface-focused strategies (coating/gradient/core-shell) face manufacturability limits because they require precise synthesis control."];
  "iJ" [label="(0,0,0) If quenching can alter non-equilibrium element/Li distributions during cooling, then it may create a less reactive surface and more homogeneous bulk charging, improving high-voltage cycling stability."];
  "n10v1" [label="(10,1,0) Limitation: these coating/gradient/core-shell strategies rely on precisely controlling synthesis conditions."];
  "n15v1" [label="(15,1,0) Practical limitation: precisely placing heteroatoms at desired lattice sites is non-trivial."];
  "n16v1" [label="(16,1,0) Practical limitation: multi-dopant doping chemistry can create a vulnerable raw-materials supply chain."];
  "n19v2" [label="(19,2,0) Calcination can drive mass transport and reaction kinetics in cathode manufacturing."];
  "n1r9" [label="(3,1,9) Prior evidence: increasing Ni content decreases capacity retention in Li[NixCoyMnz]O2."];
  "n20v2" [label="(20,2,0) Currently, typical NMC cooling rates in calcination are only a few to tens of degrees per minute (i.e., relatively slow cooling)."];
  "n21v1" [label="(21,1,0) Background knowledge: quenching (fast cooling) can produce materials far from equilibrium."];
  "n23v2" [label="(23,2,0) Prior case: quenching treatment can enhance electrochemical performance of Ni-rich positive electrodes."];
  "n24v1" [label="(24,1,0) Knowledge gap: it is largely unexplored how quenching changes surface–bulk mutual modulation and thereby affects performance."];
  "n2v1" [label="(2,1,0) Currently there is a key problem: Ni-rich cathodes have unsatisfactory cycling stability, especially at high voltages."];
  "n3v2" [label="(3,2,0) Currently there is a trend: capacity decay is accelerated as Ni content increases."];
  "n5v1" [label="(5,1,0) Surface stability is related to bulk chemo-mechanical stability during cycling."];
  "n6v1" [label="(6,1,0) There is mutual modulation between surface chemistry and bulk microstructure in NMC polycrystalline particles."];
  "n7v1" [label="(7,1,0) Existing mitigation approach: build functional surface layers (coatings) to improve surface stability."];
